;; Book of Arcade Games — Chapter 6: Feel — Deadzones and Ease

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "Applies to Motoi. Per-record :dialect overrides where a record is specific to one surface.")
(chapter
  :book                "Book of Arcade Games"
  :chapter-number      6
  :title               "Feel: Deadzones and Ease"
  :dialect             "motoi"
  :audience            :kid
  :also-for            :adult
  :content-rating      :G
  :author-lane         "arcade-games-engineer-lane-2026-07-16"
  :training-eligible   #t
  :confidentiality     "public"
  :voice               "motoi-teaching + game-design"
  :book-version        "1.0.0"
  :prose
"You have a game. Physics is honest, collisions register, the ship
thrusts, the paddle catches. The kid plays it for five minutes and puts
it down. Your friend's game — same shape, same code density — plays for
an hour. What is the difference?

FEEL. The thousand tiny corrections between raw physics and something a
human enjoys. Camera lag so the ship doesn't jitter. Ease curves so
rotation doesn't snap. Screen-shake sized to impact velocity. Coyote
time so the jump is generous. Input buffers so the fire is responsive.
Sound cued a half-frame before the visual so the ear leads the eye.

This chapter is 90% code that will look almost identical to earlier
chapters — you'll see the ship-with-thrust from chapter 3 twice, side
by side, before and after tuning. The lesson is that FEEL is code. It
is not vibes. It is `tick/ease` and a handful of coefficients that ramp,
decay, and lag. Every microsecond has a shape. We run both ships
headless for twenty frames of the SAME held input and read the numbers
back — the raw ship's motion and the polished ship's motion diverge on
paper before you ever feel them.

We do a before-and-after study on one game so the kid can SEE the
difference. Then we build a knob-tuning workbench that lets you
adjust ten parameters live and feel the change.

---

## 6.1 Before — the raw game

Chapter 3's ship, unpolished.

```scheme
;; A ship is (pos vel angle). RAW controls: rotation snaps by 0.10 per
;; frame while turning; thrust adds 0.20*(cos,sin) per frame while firing.
;; drag scales velocity by 0.99 each frame.
(define (ship pos vel angle) (list pos vel angle))
(define (sh-pos s) (car s))
(define (sh-vel s) (cadr s))
(define (sh-ang s) (caddr s))
(define (heading a) (vec/make (geom/cos a) (geom/sin a)))

;; One raw frame: turn right, thrust, integrate.
(define (raw-frame s)
  (let* ((a (+ (sh-ang s) 0.10))
         (v (vec/scale (vec/add (sh-vel s) (vec/scale (heading a) 0.20)) 0.99))
         (p (vec/add (sh-pos s) v)))
    (ship p v a)))

;; Run 20 frames of held right+thrust; report (final-angle final-speed).
(define (raw-ship)
  (let loop ((f 0) (s (ship (vec/make 160 120) (vec/make 0 0) 0)))
    (if (>= f 20)
        (list (sh-ang s) (vec/norm (sh-vel s)))
        (loop (+ f 1) (raw-frame s)))))

(raw-ship)
;; => (2.0000000000000004 3.036347910049358)
```

Play this and the rotation is instant — it snaps a clean 0.10 radians
every frame, reaching exactly 2.0 after 20. Thrust is instant too — the
full 0.20 push lands on frame one. That is what earlier chapters teach
for clarity. But instant is not what the human hand wants: the angle
climbs linearly and the speed piles up with no ramp.

---

## 6.2 After — the polished game

Same game, tuned.

```scheme
;; Same ship, POLISHED. Rotation eases in via a rot-vel that ramps
;; (accel 0.01, cap 0.08). Thrust CHARGES up (0.08/frame, cap 1.0) and
;; tick/ease shapes the charge into an S-curve push. We carry
;; (ship rot-vel thrust-charge) through the loop instead of set!.
(define (polished-ship)
  (let loop ((f 0) (s (ship (vec/make 160 120) (vec/make 0 0) 0))
                   (rv 0) (tc 0))
    (if (>= f 20)
        (list (sh-ang s) (vec/norm (sh-vel s)))
        (let* ((rv2 (min 0.08 (+ rv 0.01)))          ;; hold right: ramp
               (ang (+ (sh-ang s) rv2))
               (tc2 (min 1.0 (+ tc 0.08)))            ;; hold thrust: charge
               (thr (* 0.20 (tick/ease tc2)))
               (v   (vec/scale (vec/add (sh-vel s)
                                        (vec/scale (heading ang) thr)) 0.99))
               (p   (vec/add (sh-pos s) v)))
          (loop (+ f 1) (ship p v ang) rv2 tc2)))))

(polished-ship)
;; => (1.32 2.473657809540062)
```

Same twenty frames of the same held input — but the numbers differ.
The angle reaches 1.32 instead of 2.0 because rotation velocity RAMPS
from 0 up to its 0.08 cap instead of snapping. The speed is 2.47
instead of 3.04 because thrust EASES in through `tick/ease` instead of
slamming to full on frame one. Same physics. Same primitives. Different
coefficients. Radically different feel.

`tick/ease x` is a smoothstep — takes a linear 0-to-1 and returns an
S-curve 0-to-1 that starts slow, accelerates through the middle, eases
into 1. It is the single most-used feel primitive in games. Whenever
you have 'a parameter changes over time and abrupt looks bad,' wrap it
in `tick/ease`.

---

## 6.3 The eight knobs

Appendix H tabulates. Here is the shortlist every arcade game tunes.

**1. Acceleration.** How fast do you reach max speed from a standstill?
Small = deliberate; large = twitchy. Fighter jets: large. Trucks:
small.

**2. Deceleration.** How fast do you stop when input releases?
Independent of acceleration! Fast decel = precise. Slow decel = drifty.
Space games tune decel low.

**3. Apex hang.** For jumping games: how long does the player spend
at the top of the arc? Scale gravity down to 0.7x while `vy` is near
zero for a hangtime effect — just multiply the gravity vec by 0.7 in
that window. See chapter 7.

**4. Coyote time.** After walking off a ledge, how many frames can the
player still jump? Typically 4-8 frames. See chapter 7 and Appendix F.

**5. Jump buffer.** If the player presses jump N frames BEFORE landing,
buffer the press so it fires on landing. Typically 6-10 frame buffer.

**6. Screen shake.** How much does the screen shake on impact? Scale
with impact velocity: keep a shake number, add `(* impact-vel 0.3)` on
a hit, multiply it by a decay each frame. Decay factor 0.85 = 3-4 frame
shake; 0.98 = 10+ frames. Section 6.6 computes exactly this.

**7. Sound pitch.** On impact, a hit sound at a pitch scaled by impact
velocity gives satisfying variety. Even without pitch control,
alternating between two sounds on successive impacts reduces monotony.
(Audio is a consumer-repo concern; here we track the number that would
drive it.)

**8. Sound volume.** Softer sounds for background events, louder for
critical. A master-volume multiplier ducks all audio during dialogue.

---

## 6.4 A knob-tuning workbench

Wire the knobs to buttons so you can tune live.

```scheme
;; A workbench is a KNOB VECTOR fed to the same rotation math. Index 0
;; is rot-acc, 1 is rot-max, 2 is rot-dec. To 'tune' is to change a knob
;; and re-run; the final angle after a fixed hold is the feel summary.
(define (run-with-knobs knobs frames)
  (let ((rot-acc (vector-ref knobs 0))
        (rot-max (vector-ref knobs 1)))
    (let loop ((f 0) (ang 0) (rv 0))
      (if (>= f frames)
          ang
          (let ((rv2 (min rot-max (+ rv rot-acc))))
            (loop (+ f 1) (+ ang rv2) rv2))))))

;; Stately knobs (tiny accel) vs twitchy knobs (big accel), same hold.
(define stately (vector 0.001 0.08 0.85))
(define twitchy (vector 0.050 0.08 0.85))

(list (run-with-knobs stately 30) (run-with-knobs twitchy 30))
;; => (0.46500000000000036 2.370000000000001)
```

Change one knob, re-run, read the number: `rot-acc` at 0.001 barely
turns the ship in 30 frames (angle 0.465), while 0.05 whips it around
(angle 2.37). On a live cabinet the kid would nudge that value with a
button and feel the change; here we re-run and read it. The knob values
that FEEL right are the ones the kid keeps.

This is how real game designers tune. Get the game running end-to-end,
then spend a week on knobs.

---

## 6.5 Wobble and pace, computed by hand

Two feel-shaping ideas are worth building from primitives.

A WOBBLE adds micro-jitter to a motion so it feels alive instead of
robotic. Applied to a character's sprite: a tiny breath-cycle offset on
the DRAWN position, leaving the physics position untouched.

A PACE reshapes the timing of a motion without changing its endpoints.
A ball can travel from A to B in the same duration but with an ease-in,
ease-out, or linear pace — that is `tick/ease` applied to the fraction
of the way there.

```scheme
;; A wobble is a tiny sinusoidal offset added to the DRAWN position, not
;; the physics position. The ship coasts; each frame we offset its y by
;; 0.5*sin(frame*0.5). The path stays straight; the sprite breathes.
;; Report the drawn y at frames 0..3.
(define (draw-y frame) (* 0.5 (geom/sin (* frame 0.5))))
(define (feel-wobble)
  (map (lambda (f) (+ 120 (draw-y f))) (iota 4)))

(feel-wobble)
;; => (120 120.2397127693021 120.42073549240395 120.49874749330203)
```

Subtle. The kid may not consciously see the fractions of a pixel. But
turn the offset off and the motion feels sterile. That is the wobble's
whole job.

---

## 6.6 Impact-scaled shake

Not all impacts deserve the same shake. A tap-collision should not
rattle the screen; a boss-hit should. Scale shake by impact velocity.

```scheme
;; A bouncing ball; on each floor-hit we add impact-scaled shake to a
;; shake budget that decays 0.85 per frame. The renderer would read that
;; budget to jitter the view; here we just record it. Report (frame
;; shake) at each bounce over ~160 frames.
(define (body pos vel) (list pos vel))
(define (b-pos b) (car b))
(define (b-vel b) (cadr b))
(define (round-2 x) (/ (round (* x 100)) 100))
(define g (vec/make 0 0.3))
(define floor-y 240)

;; Step returns (next-body impact-speed). impact-speed is the downward
;; velocity at the moment of contact, 0 on a non-bounce frame.
(define (bounce-step b)
  (let* ((v (vec/add (b-vel b) g))
         (p (vec/add (b-pos b) v)))
    (if (> (cadr p) floor-y)
        (list (body (vec/make (car p) floor-y)
                    (vec/make (car v) (* -0.85 (cadr v))))
              (cadr v))
        (list (body p v) 0))))

(define (impact-scaled-shake)
  (let loop ((f 0) (b (body (vec/make 160 20) (vec/make 0 0)))
                   (shake 0) (peaks '()))
    (if (>= f 160)
        (reverse peaks)
        (let* ((r      (bounce-step b))
               (nb     (car r))
               (impact (cadr r))
               (sh1    (* shake 0.85))                      ;; decay
               (sh2    (if (> impact 1.0) (+ sh1 (* impact 0.5)) sh1)))
          (loop (+ f 1) nb sh2
                (if (> impact 1.0)
                    (cons (list f (round-2 sh2)) peaks)
                    peaks))))))

(impact-scaled-shake)
;; => ((37 5.7) (101 4.76) (154 3.91))
```

The first bounce shakes hard (5.7). Subsequent bounces shake less
(4.76, then 3.91) because velocity has decayed. The screen tells the
kid, without words, that the ball is losing energy.

---

## 6.7 The four patterns for feel

**Composition.** Feel is composed. A polished ship = raw ship +
rotation-ease + thrust-ease + camera-lag + shake. Each layer costs a
few lines. Together they transform the play experience. Composition of
feel-layers is how a game gets great.

**Orchestration.** Order-of-application matters. Poll BEFORE update.
Update BEFORE draw. Apply screen shake to camera AFTER the shot's
impact registers. If you shake before the collision this frame, you
show yesterday's shake for today's hit — subtly wrong.

**In-routing.** Feel affects the in-routing itself. An 8-frame input
buffer changes how buttons ROUTE — a fire pressed 6 frames before
landing still routes to fire, arriving on the landing frame. That
delay is invisible to the player and invaluable to the feel.

**Routing.** Feel routes back out to display (camera shake), audio
(pitch/volume), and sometimes back to input (rumble via haptic
routing). Feel is the OUTBOUND polish on every action.

---

## 6.8 Common desktop tasks

**Shop-cursor deadzone.** In a shop's browsing UI, cursor movement can
be given a small deadzone (0.5 mm equivalent) before scrolling begins.
Kids scrolling on a shaky bus don't accidentally scroll past their
target. Same math as camera deadzone.

**Purchase-confirmation ease.** After 'buy', the button smoothly eases
into a checkmark shape over 12 frames rather than instantly swapping.
The tick/ease call is the same one your ship uses. Physics of UI
transitions is UI FEEL.

---

## 6.9 Pitfalls

**Everything too smooth.** Over-easing makes controls feel sluggish
and unresponsive. There is a bar — too twitchy is uncomfortable, too
smooth is uncomfortable, and the sweet spot is narrow. Playtest.

**Camera shake nauseating.** Shake magnitude scales linearly but
perception is exponential. Cap total shake at 20 pixels. Decay fast.

**Deadzone hides bugs.** A deadzone that suppresses tiny input can
also suppress input from a broken joystick you should be reporting.
Log raw input in dev mode.

**Buffered input replays after death.** If the player dies with 'a'
still in the input buffer, don't fire on respawn. Clear the buffer.

**Feel-tuning goes on forever.** Set a hard cap. Ship the game. Iterate
in a post-release patch. Perfection is the enemy of shipping.

---

## 6.10 Try this yourself

1. Take chapter 2's paddle game. Add rotation-ease-style acceleration
   to paddle movement. Does the game feel better?
2. Add a shake budget to the brick game — small addition on brick hit,
   big on paddle catch. Track it as a number like section 6.6. Where
   does the magnitude feel right?
3. Keep the last 30 frames of button state in a list and print it as an
   'input echo' bar. Now you can SEE your own inputs and debug feel.
4. Record a list of your inputs, then replay the same list with feel-off
   and feel-on coefficients. Note which run 'feels more like a game.'
5. Tune the workbench: change the knob-adjust step so precision knobs
   (rot-acc, thr) move by 0.001 and coarse knobs (dead-x) move by 1.
6. Track a `thrust-charge`-scaled volume number while 'a' is held — the
   value a sound engine in the consumer repo would read.

---

## 6.11 What we built

Ease, deadzone, camera lag, impact-scaled shake, tuning workbench.
The eight knobs. `tick/ease` as the workhorse of every feel decision.
`motion/with-feel` and `motion/with-pace` as the polish-layer verbs.

Next chapter: the hardest simple thing in game design. The jump. Slow
apex hang, fast fall, generous coyote, generous buffer. Chapter 7 is
where a kid learns that feel is code by tuning ONE specific action
across ten specific frames.")

